// Transitive delegatecall-reachable selfdestruct: a `SELFDESTRUCT` that
// executed successfully somewhere *beneath* a `DELEGATECALL` frame in
// the call tree, not necessarily as its direct child.
//
// Why this is needed alongside `delegatecall_reachable_selfdestruct`
// (Milestone 9): that pattern's `parent_kind: Delegate` attribute only
// checks the SELFDESTRUCT's *immediate* parent frame. But the Parity
// multisig library shape it models does not require the SELFDESTRUCT
// to be called directly by the DELEGATECALL — any code path reachable
// from inside the delegatecall's borrowed execution context still
// executes against the caller's (proxy's) storage, and a SELFDESTRUCT
// anywhere in that subtree still destroys the library contract whose
// code is actually running. A realistic shape this misses:
// `DELEGATECALL → CALL → SELFDESTRUCT` — the delegatecalled library
// makes one more internal call (e.g. to a helper/internal function
// exposed as its own call frame) before self-destructing.
// `parent_kind: Delegate` reports `0 candidate match(es)` on that
// shape, a false negative for a genuinely dangerous trace.
//
// How this is expressed: `call(...)` gained a new optional
// `ancestor_kind` attribute this milestone (`crates/dsl/src/schema.rs`),
// generalizing `parent_kind` from a one-hop parent check to a walk of
// the *entire* ancestor chain (`fact_model::Call::parent`, followed
// repeatedly back to the root) — see
// `crates/matcher/src/predicate.rs`'s `has_ancestor_of_kind`.
// `call(kind: SelfDestruct, succeeded: true, ancestor_kind: Delegate)`
// is a single, self-contained evidence clause: it demands *some*
// ancestor of the SELFDESTRUCT — not necessarily its direct parent —
// be a DELEGATECALL (or its historical predecessor CALLCODE, exactly
// as `parent_kind` already treats them, via the same
// `call_kind_matches` helper both attributes share).
//
// No fact-model extension was needed: `Call::parent: Option<CallId>`
// already existed and already supported the one-hop walk
// `parent_kind` used; this milestone's only additive change is the DSL
// attribute and the matcher predicate filter that walks the existing
// field transitively instead of once.
//
// Relationship to the Milestone 9 pattern: this pattern strictly
// subsumes `delegatecall_reachable_selfdestruct` in coverage (every
// direct-child case is also an ancestor-chain case), but the two are
// kept as separate patterns rather than one replacing the other —
// `delegatecall_reachable_selfdestruct` remains valuable as the
// tightest, most immediately actionable signature (the delegatecall
// and the destructive call are adjacent, easiest for a reviewer to
// audit), while this pattern trades a little precision of "where
// exactly" for recall across deeper call trees. Both share the same
// `DelegatecallReachableSelfdestruct` family and the same taxonomy
// mappings (SWC-106 / SCWE-038) deliberately: they are the same
// underlying finding at two different structural granularities, not
// two different vulnerability classes.
//
// Scope note: identical to `delegatecall_reachable_selfdestruct` —
// this pattern cannot verify whether the delegatecall itself was
// authorized, or whether the library's own SELFDESTRUCT function
// lacked an access modifier in source. It surfaces the structural
// shape a reviewer needs to check, not a proof that access control was
// missing.
pattern delegatecall_reachable_selfdestruct_transitive version 1 {
    family: DelegatecallReachableSelfdestruct
    severity: Critical
    tags: ["selfdestruct", "delegatecall", "access-control", "irrevocable", "library", "transitive"]
    references: ["SWC-106", "SCWE-038"]

    evidence {
        required destruct_beneath_delegatecall: call(
            kind: SelfDestruct,
            succeeded: true,
            ancestor_kind: Delegate
        )
    }

    constraint: destruct_beneath_delegatecall
}
